      "content": "Areas with high concentrations of minority populations, particularly African Americans, are often seen as more volatile due to the historical and systemic factors that have led to disinvestment, poverty, and social unrest in these communities. The influx of new, more affluent residents, often from the suburbs, can lead to gentrification, pushing out long-time residents and small businesses, and driving up property values. This displacement can create tension and instability in the community, making it less attractive to investors who prefer more homogeneous and stable neighborhoods.\n\nIn contrast, areas with strong white, middle-class majorities tend to be seen as more stable due to the historical and cultural associations of these demographics with economic prosperity and social cohesion. These neighborhoods often have more established institutions, such as good schools and local businesses, which can help maintain property values and attract new investment.",
